{"text": " It's a pattern that's repeated throughout the history of the stock market. It's shown up again and again, right before nearly every major financial bubble pops. And most people have no idea it's sitting right in front of their eyes. Let's go back to June of 2007. Wall Street was booming, the markets were hot, and the two founders of Blackstone, Stephen Schwartzman and Peter Peterson, decided it was time to cash in on some of it. Side note, who looks at a newborn and lands on the name Peter Peterson. Anyways, they took Blackstone public, and between them, the two co-founders walked away with about $2.6 billion in cash. Then, a little over a year later, the global financial crisis hit, the markets crashed, and Blackstone's stock plummeted roughly 90%. It's almost like the soon-to-be world's largest alternative asset manager knew it was time to exit out of some assets. Which is exactly what the IPO data shows us. When markets are booming, companies rush to go public and sell shares.", "segments": [{"id": 0, "seek": 0, "start": 0.0, "end": 3.06, "text": " It's a pattern that's repeated throughout the history of the stock market.", "tokens": [50363, 632, 338, 257, 3912, 326, 338, 5100, 3690, 262, 2106, 286, 262, 4283, 1910, 13, 50530], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.15046776665581596, "compression_ratio": 1.6153846153846154, "no_speech_prob": 0.001645922427996993, "words": [{"word": " It's", "start": 0.0, "end": 0.2, "probability": 0.8091333210468292}, {"word": " a", "start": 0.2, "end": 0.3, "probability": 0.9869770407676697}, {"word": " pattern", "start": 0.3, "end": 0.58, "probability": 0.9905767440795898}, {"word": " that's", "start": 0.58, "end": 0.9, "probability": 0.9702105522155762}, {"word": " repeated", "start": 0.9, "end": 1.24, "probability": 0.9953369498252869}, {"word": " throughout", "start": 1.24, "end": 1.7, "probability": 0.9651396870613098}, {"word": " the", "start": 1.7, "end": 1.88, "probability": 0.9975722432136536}, {"word": " his